  {
   "old": "\\caption{Results for each cell, one model under one prompt condition. \\emph{Share} is the replication share, the fraction of used slots spent re-testing a candidate already ordered. \\emph{Excl.\\ ref.} leaves out re-orders of the reference candidate, whose score the agent is given in advance. \\emph{Distinct} is the mean number of different candidates measured per campaign, and \\emph{fitness} the mean true fitness of the final pick. The last three columns compare each cell with the guessing policy at its best setting, which measures 25.65 distinct candidates and reaches 7.89. \\emph{Tested} is how much further the best candidate the agents measured falls short of the global best. \\emph{Picked} is the change in how far their pick falls short of the best they measured, negative where they picked better. \\emph{Net} is the sum, the agents' deficit in final-pick fitness; rounded parts may not add to it exactly. Figure~\\ref{fig:ladder} gives 95\\% intervals on fitness.}",
   "new": "\\caption{Results for each cell, one model under one prompt condition. \\emph{Share} is the replication share, the fraction of used slots spent re-testing a candidate already ordered. \\emph{Excl.\\ ref.} leaves out re-orders of the reference candidate, whose score the agent is given in advance. \\emph{Distinct} is the mean number of different candidates measured per campaign, and \\emph{fitness} the mean true fitness of the final pick. The last three columns compare each cell with the guessing policy at its best setting, which measures 28.94 distinct candidates and reaches 7.87. \\emph{Tested} is how much further the best candidate the agents measured falls short of the global best. \\emph{Picked} is the change in how far their pick falls short of the best they measured, negative where they picked better. \\emph{Net} is the sum, the agents' deficit in final-pick fitness; rounded parts may not add to it exactly. Figure~\\ref{fig:ladder} gives 95\\% intervals on fitness.}"
  },
